WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.343 --> 00:00:01.864
- He's an American businessman,

00:00:01.864 --> 00:00:04.585
CEO and founder of Under Armour.

00:00:04.585 --> 00:00:07.651
- He has a net worth
of 3.2 billion dollars.

00:00:07.651 --> 00:00:10.142
- In 2012, he was named number three

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.643
on Forbes 40 Under 40 list.

00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:13.737
- He's Kevin Plank,

00:00:13.737 --> 00:00:17.237
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:20.336 --> 00:00:21.602
- I think we control our own destiny

00:00:21.602 --> 00:00:23.740
and so, whether David makes it or not

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.756
has nothing to do with the
size or might of Goliath.

00:00:26.756 --> 00:00:28.271
And if you're thinking like that,

00:00:28.271 --> 00:00:30.719
you'll probably always
be able to find a reason

00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:32.342
and frankly, an excuse

00:00:32.342 --> 00:00:35.395
and some lame answer as
to why you can't win.

00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:37.132
Because the markets aren't good.

00:00:37.132 --> 00:00:40.895
I was in the middle of this
through 2000 and the first crash

00:00:40.895 --> 00:00:41.728
and people are going,

00:00:41.728 --> 00:00:42.934
"Market's terrible, market's terrible!"

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:44.712
I'm like, "I'm sellin' stretchy t-shirts."

00:00:44.712 --> 00:00:48.268
Nothing to do with the financial crisis.

00:00:48.268 --> 00:00:49.464
Sports are going to be played

00:00:49.464 --> 00:00:51.745
and kids will wear it, or they won't.

00:00:51.745 --> 00:00:54.667
And I think anybody can look and find,

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.367
but I challenge all of us to say,

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.428
"Stop finding reasons to
find and make excuses."

00:01:00.428 --> 00:01:03.604
That we affectionately, at
Under Armour, call loser talk.

00:01:03.604 --> 00:01:05.430
And, I don't know.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.573
You've got to just will it

00:01:07.573 --> 00:01:10.668
and focus on making it happen.

00:01:10.668 --> 00:01:11.768
There's a saying,

00:01:11.768 --> 00:01:13.620
it was actually attributed
to the chairman of Sony

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.275
several years ago.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:19.442
And it said that, perfection
is the enemy of innovation.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.307
Perfection is the enemy of innovation.

00:01:23.307 --> 00:01:25.003
And what that effectively means is that,

00:01:25.003 --> 00:01:26.463
how many entrepreneurs
are sitting out there

00:01:26.463 --> 00:01:27.562
with a great idea,

00:01:27.562 --> 00:01:29.696
tinkering in their garage right now,

00:01:29.696 --> 00:01:31.605
waiting to get this concept out there,

00:01:31.605 --> 00:01:34.138
waiting to get the idea out there.

00:01:34.138 --> 00:01:36.975
But it's not quite perfect yet.

00:01:36.975 --> 00:01:38.569
The criteria that we use at Under Armour

00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:40.013
whenever we look at a product,

00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:41.920
or whenever we decide to do something,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.963
is that it must be better

00:01:42.963 --> 00:01:45.193
than what's currently
available on the market.

00:01:45.193 --> 00:01:47.756
It may not be the perfect
vision of what we have,

00:01:47.756 --> 00:01:49.288
but sometimes you have to get in the game

00:01:49.288 --> 00:01:51.087
in order to make it improve.

00:01:51.087 --> 00:01:52.997
Don't ever settle.

00:01:52.997 --> 00:01:55.609
Don't ever let a product just be okay,

00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:57.871
or just barely good enough.

00:01:57.871 --> 00:02:01.052
But I challenge all of
us to think in that way,

00:02:01.052 --> 00:02:02.272
as to good.

00:02:02.272 --> 00:02:03.214
Leader is required.

00:02:03.214 --> 00:02:05.539
It's in the word, it's to lead.

00:02:05.539 --> 00:02:06.852
And it's to lead by example

00:02:06.852 --> 00:02:08.525
and it's to have and set a vision,

00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:10.416
and all those kind of
things that you need.

00:02:10.416 --> 00:02:11.459
But it's to make sure that you never limit

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:13.692
the idea or the opportunity.

00:02:13.692 --> 00:02:16.024
What I find myself doing
more often than not,

00:02:16.024 --> 00:02:18.736
is ensuring that we have
enough runway in front of us.

00:02:18.736 --> 00:02:20.910
Ensuring that we are thinking
about things big enough.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.282
Because, you can become complacent.

00:02:23.282 --> 00:02:24.845
Your job is to push your company,

00:02:24.845 --> 00:02:26.047
but it's not just to show up and say,

00:02:26.047 --> 00:02:27.514
"I want 20% more."

00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:29.225
More importantly you need
to set an expectation,

00:02:29.225 --> 00:02:31.217
and set a path and a vision

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:33.851
that allows people to achieve
that at the same time.

00:02:33.851 --> 00:02:37.159
I can remember those early
days when you want to be big.

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:38.743
You're constantly projecting yourself

00:02:38.743 --> 00:02:39.957
as the company you're going to be.

00:02:39.957 --> 00:02:41.514
And whether it was 1996,

00:02:41.514 --> 00:02:44.826
and we're starting out with 17
thousand dollars in revenues

00:02:44.826 --> 00:02:47.988
and you've got that, "We're
going to beat those bad guys."

00:02:47.988 --> 00:02:49.043
Whatever your industry is,

00:02:49.043 --> 00:02:50.849
whoever the big one you can point at.

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:52.663
And it's good to have a nemesis.

00:02:52.663 --> 00:02:54.041
Luke Skywalker wouldn't be him

00:02:54.041 --> 00:02:55.218
if he didn't have Darth Vader.

00:02:55.218 --> 00:02:57.123
And so you need to decide and define,

00:02:57.123 --> 00:02:58.658
who's your Darth Vader.

00:02:58.658 --> 00:03:00.833
And then you typically put a,

00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:02.906
put them over the dart board
and you throw darts at them,

00:03:02.906 --> 00:03:04.139
you think about them.

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:04.972
But you understand

00:03:04.972 --> 00:03:06.340
that in order for you to be successful

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.789
it's got nothing to do with anybody else.

00:03:08.789 --> 00:03:11.406
And frankly, the games that
we all are typically playing

00:03:11.406 --> 00:03:13.243
are not zero-sum games either.

00:03:13.243 --> 00:03:15.230
And so in order for us to win,

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.526
which is still part of
the ethos and philosophy

00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:19.100
we take at Under Armour today,

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.962
it's not a matter, necessarily,
of someone else loosing.

00:03:21.962 --> 00:03:24.963
In sporting goods, the
pie was about this big.

00:03:24.963 --> 00:03:27.397
And you'd look and people
were constantly trying to take

00:03:27.397 --> 00:03:29.507
shares from each other, back and forth.

00:03:29.507 --> 00:03:30.989
And when we got into it,

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:33.125
and the thing that allowed
us for our breakthrough,

00:03:33.125 --> 00:03:36.067
was we effectively went
and made our own pie.

00:03:36.067 --> 00:03:38.541
And with that we created this new category

00:03:38.541 --> 00:03:40.731
and it gave us the legs to think about,

00:03:40.731 --> 00:03:43.903
not necessarily how we
compared to somebody else,

00:03:43.903 --> 00:03:45.155
but the opportunity that we had.

00:03:45.155 --> 00:03:47.753
I think when comparing what we were doing

00:03:47.753 --> 00:03:50.647
versus compared to what we
felt the opportunity was.

00:03:50.647 --> 00:03:52.735
I was lucky enough to
not listen to people.

00:03:52.735 --> 00:03:54.525
I was lucky enough to use this statement

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:57.041
that I framed my thinking back then,

00:03:57.041 --> 00:03:59.072
and hopefully still today.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:01.422
I was always smart enough

00:04:01.422 --> 00:04:03.303
to be naive enough

00:04:03.303 --> 00:04:06.236
to not know we could not accomplish.

00:04:06.236 --> 00:04:07.846
I was always smart
enough to be naive enough

00:04:07.846 --> 00:04:09.529
to not know we could not accomplish.

00:04:09.529 --> 00:04:11.200
Why not us?

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.610
Because anybody smart I talked to said,

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:13.443
"Are you crazy?

00:04:13.443 --> 00:04:15.904
"You're going to go against
these behemoths in this industry?

00:04:15.904 --> 00:04:17.317
"You know how big these other brands are?

00:04:17.317 --> 00:04:19.709
"The guys out West, the guys in Germany?

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.210
"It's a dangerous category."

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.030
The fact is I didn't care.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:24.863
All I knew is that

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:26.871
I was going to make the
worlds greatest t-shirt.

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:27.704
And it started out

00:04:27.704 --> 00:04:29.433
making the worlds greatest
t-shirt for football players.

00:04:29.433 --> 00:04:30.778
That quickly grew to baseball players,

00:04:30.778 --> 00:04:33.178
lacrosse players, hockey players.

00:04:33.178 --> 00:04:34.603
On and on and on.

00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:36.559
Great product always wins

00:04:36.559 --> 00:04:38.827
and we are fortunate to have that.

00:04:38.827 --> 00:04:40.866
And so the hardest thing
that any organization has

00:04:40.866 --> 00:04:42.723
is getting the people right, right?

00:04:42.723 --> 00:04:44.090
And so, I'm very fortunate

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.082
because the first decision that I made

00:04:46.082 --> 00:04:49.544
was to hire my first partner,
which was Kip Fulks, right?

00:04:49.544 --> 00:04:50.424
And getting Kip in,

00:04:50.424 --> 00:04:53.065
and then I trusted that Kip
would hire two great people.

00:04:53.065 --> 00:04:54.867
And they would hire three great people.

00:04:54.867 --> 00:04:55.700
And on and on and on.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.663
Along the way, you get to establish

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.433
and you get to build a culture.

00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:00.963
And that's something that takes

00:05:00.963 --> 00:05:02.824
a long, long time to do.

00:05:02.824 --> 00:05:04.861
And, it's something that
you better be good at

00:05:04.861 --> 00:05:07.012
because you're never done.

00:05:07.012 --> 00:05:08.859
Particularly in a growing company.

00:05:08.859 --> 00:05:11.846
I would tell you, that the number one key

00:05:11.846 --> 00:05:13.107
to Under Armour's success,

00:05:13.107 --> 00:05:17.892
to any new company's success,
plain and simple, is focus.

00:05:17.892 --> 00:05:20.482
And the thing lost in the first ten years

00:05:20.482 --> 00:05:24.211
that we don't get credit
for at Under Armor was that,

00:05:24.211 --> 00:05:27.060
from 1996 to really, 2000,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.766
we had one product.

00:05:28.766 --> 00:05:30.518
Great entrepreneurs take
one product and say,

00:05:30.518 --> 00:05:32.880
we're going to be great for this.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.653
And most importantly, we're
going to be famous for one thing.

00:05:37.653 --> 00:05:39.660
And that is the greatest
advice I can give you

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:40.695
because so often,

00:05:40.695 --> 00:05:43.587
you get caught up with trying
to keep up with the Jones'

00:05:43.587 --> 00:05:45.344
and going, "But they're doing
this, and they're doing that."

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:49.263
And it's like, they're
also 150 times your size.

00:05:49.263 --> 00:05:50.937
You're not in the same league.

00:05:50.937 --> 00:05:52.451
You need to become great at one thing.

00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:53.937
And that's where, at Under Armour,

00:05:53.937 --> 00:05:55.809
when the first five years, it was.

00:05:55.809 --> 00:05:56.653
Where today,

00:05:56.653 --> 00:05:59.273
we're on, I think it's over
the 60th different iteration

00:05:59.273 --> 00:06:00.106
of that first

00:06:00.106 --> 00:06:04.235
short sleeved, wicking,
compression t-shirt that I made.

00:06:04.235 --> 00:06:05.713
And so often you see people going,

00:06:05.713 --> 00:06:06.752
"You've got to make a long sleeve.

00:06:06.752 --> 00:06:08.123
"You've got to make one for womens.

00:06:08.123 --> 00:06:09.420
"You've got to make one here."

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.147
And I was like, "I have to
make this product great."

00:06:13.147 --> 00:06:14.950
And we literally sold it and made it,

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:16.597
and made it over and over.

00:06:16.597 --> 00:06:18.790
And it was never frankly, done.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:21.141
And that's what allowed
us, from that t-shirt.

00:06:21.141 --> 00:06:22.584
And like, "You should make
shoes, you should make shoes,

00:06:22.584 --> 00:06:23.979
"you should make shoes."

00:06:23.979 --> 00:06:25.290
And I'm going, "We're
not ready to make shoes."

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.257
And it wasn't until 2006 when
we finally launched shoes.

00:06:28.257 --> 00:06:29.853
Ten years into our company.

00:06:29.853 --> 00:06:32.283
And then frankly, it's
not even really until now,

00:06:32.283 --> 00:06:34.978
and getting better, ten years
after beginning to make them,

00:06:34.978 --> 00:06:37.085
and eight years after selling them,

00:06:37.085 --> 00:06:39.384
where I think we're really
beginning to see scale

00:06:39.384 --> 00:06:41.777
in ability to be great in footwear.

00:06:41.777 --> 00:06:44.912
And it took us becoming
famous in one thing first.

00:06:44.912 --> 00:06:47.300
Before you just start running
off on a number of ideas

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.873
that aren't the most important thing

00:06:48.873 --> 00:06:50.966
to the success of your business.

00:06:50.966 --> 00:06:53.781
You know speed and the ability
to make fast decisions.

00:06:53.781 --> 00:06:55.097
You know, smarts is something

00:06:55.097 --> 00:06:57.694
you never want to turn your back on.

00:06:57.694 --> 00:06:59.964
But if I had to choose, I
would probably take speed.

00:06:59.964 --> 00:07:01.530
Especially in starting a business

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:02.599
and being an entrepreneur.

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:04.024
There's so many great ideas right now

00:07:04.024 --> 00:07:05.973
that are sitting in a
basement or an attic,

00:07:05.973 --> 00:07:07.730
where the entrepreneur
is just timid or afraid

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.321
to get out to market.

00:07:09.321 --> 00:07:10.444
'Cause they think it's not perfect.

00:07:10.444 --> 00:07:12.438
It's not right, it's not ready to go.

00:07:12.438 --> 00:07:13.802
And the one you learn in business

00:07:13.802 --> 00:07:14.635
is that version two

00:07:14.635 --> 00:07:16.484
is always going to be
better than version one.

00:07:16.484 --> 00:07:19.530
You can say, "I want to
wait, I want to hold back.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:20.671
"I'm just not ready."

00:07:20.671 --> 00:07:22.334
But to go and to move forward.

00:07:22.334 --> 00:07:24.355
And I've had to make decision as a company

00:07:24.355 --> 00:07:26.766
that I didn't believe were
always the perfect decisions.

00:07:26.766 --> 00:07:27.876
But I believe they were probably

00:07:27.876 --> 00:07:30.998
the best decisions for us as
a company and us as a brand.

00:07:30.998 --> 00:07:32.730
And sometimes that better decision,

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.531
is just making a decision.

00:07:34.531 --> 00:07:36.903
That it is absolutely,
in any good company,

00:07:36.903 --> 00:07:39.747
there must be a
fight-like-family mentality.

00:07:39.747 --> 00:07:40.794
Where you sit at that table

00:07:40.794 --> 00:07:42.193
and every idea is out there.

00:07:42.193 --> 00:07:43.840
And whether it's me pushing them,

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.960
'cause I'm tryin' to get my company ready

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.366
for the scale of growth
that we're about to see.

00:07:48.366 --> 00:07:50.639
And tryin' to prepare and get
yourself in front of that.

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:51.868
Because many of you will be faced,

00:07:51.868 --> 00:07:54.531
where, typically in any business,

00:07:54.531 --> 00:07:55.516
one is lagging the other.

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:57.287
I say one, is that you
sort of have front end

00:07:57.287 --> 00:07:58.685
and you have back end.

00:07:58.685 --> 00:08:00.214
And there this constant natural tension

00:08:00.214 --> 00:08:01.250
between people saying,

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.948
"We have all the facilities,
we can do it, we have to."

00:08:03.948 --> 00:08:07.152
And I'm one of those
that believes in, like,

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:08.677
you've got to sell it first.

00:08:08.677 --> 00:08:09.805
You've got to push it

00:08:09.805 --> 00:08:11.225
and we're going to figure
out how to make it.

00:08:11.225 --> 00:08:12.967
We're going to figure
out how to get there.

00:08:12.967 --> 00:08:14.399
And there's times where
we've gotten too big

00:08:14.399 --> 00:08:15.232
on the other side.

00:08:15.232 --> 00:08:17.167
But the bigger box, the
bigger warehouse you build,

00:08:17.167 --> 00:08:18.924
you're typically filling it
with stuff you don't need.

00:08:18.924 --> 00:08:21.368
And if there's any two things
going to kill you in business,

00:08:21.368 --> 00:08:23.424
it is people and it is inventory.

00:08:23.424 --> 00:08:25.626
And be incredibly
sensible to both of them.

00:08:25.626 --> 00:08:27.472
And for the entrepreneur
in here that may be

00:08:27.472 --> 00:08:29.468
in a fund raise or going in a fund raise,

00:08:29.468 --> 00:08:30.757
or thinking about a fund raise

00:08:30.757 --> 00:08:33.429
and it's the worst thing I
think I see is, companies,

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:36.829
new companies that become
perpetual money raisers.

00:08:36.829 --> 00:08:38.659
If you need money, why don't
you do yourself a favor

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:40.582
you keep the equity,

00:08:40.582 --> 00:08:43.031
and if you need money, go sell
the stuff in your warehouse.

00:08:43.031 --> 00:08:44.631
Go sell your product.

00:08:44.631 --> 00:08:45.957
And if you can't sell your product

00:08:45.957 --> 00:08:48.544
then there's probably something
wrong with the business.

00:08:48.544 --> 00:08:51.061
Because there's nothing
more fundamental than,

00:08:51.061 --> 00:08:54.028
is someone willing to take
cash out of their pocket

00:08:54.028 --> 00:08:57.320
and put it in your hand,
and receive your good.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:58.724
And if you can't answer that question of,

00:08:58.724 --> 00:09:00.703
does your product sell,

00:09:00.703 --> 00:09:03.406
you need to have a faster conversation.

00:09:03.406 --> 00:09:05.164
If you are sitting on an idea

00:09:05.164 --> 00:09:06.434
whether it's on your laptop,

00:09:06.434 --> 00:09:07.282
whether it's in your garage,

00:09:07.282 --> 00:09:08.247
whether it's in your basement,

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:09.904
whether it's in the back of your car,

00:09:09.904 --> 00:09:11.859
give it a shot, right?

00:09:11.859 --> 00:09:13.155
And the best advice I ever received,

00:09:13.155 --> 00:09:14.852
find out if your product can sell.

00:09:14.852 --> 00:09:16.805
Don't let anybody talk you out of it.

00:09:16.805 --> 00:09:17.732
Give it a chance.

00:09:17.732 --> 00:09:18.746
Try.

00:09:18.746 --> 00:09:19.579
Make it happen.

00:09:19.579 --> 00:09:21.604
This is our greatest asset as a Nation,

00:09:21.604 --> 00:09:23.871
and one that we have to
continue to encourage.

00:09:23.871 --> 00:09:25.279
You don't have to go broke,

00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:26.741
you don't have to bet everything on it.

00:09:26.741 --> 00:09:28.517
But you can start one day at a time,

00:09:28.517 --> 00:09:29.426
taking a chance,

